WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.840
A year ago this week the World Health&nbsp;
Organization declared COVID-19 a pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.520
While China deserves primary blame for the&nbsp;
devastation, the WHO was a key player unwilling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.760
blame Beijing for its lack of transparency. The WHO&nbsp;
failed in its primary responsibility: to warn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:27.760
world of the pandemic risk. Even while the disease&nbsp;
forced lockdowns across China, the WHO hesitated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.040
to declare COVID a public health emergency. And
when the group's director, who was, by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.800
Beijing's candidate for the position, finally made&nbsp;
the declaration, he echoed China's talking points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.320
praising the country, while ignoring that Beijing&nbsp;
silenced doctors. To regain credibility it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:50.480
vital that the WHO implement reforms, including&nbsp;
determining what went wrong through a timely and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.600
impartial investigation, taking the process&nbsp;
from declaring a public health emergency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.200
out of the hands of bureaucrats, insulating the&nbsp;
health emergency program from political pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:08.068
and matching resources to priorities. Absent&nbsp;
reform it will be each nation for itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.720
Should the united states do more to&nbsp;
reform the World Health Organization?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.800
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know&nbsp;
what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.720
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

